---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
import { memberConfig } from '../../lib/members/config';

const list = 'mt-3 mb-5 pl-5 list-disc flex flex-col gap-2';
const boxList = 'mt-0 mb-0 pl-5 list-disc flex flex-col gap-2';
const box = 'group mt-4 rounded-md border border-border-soft bg-surface-tint px-5 py-4';
const summary = 'flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 font-display text-base font-semibold text-text-heading';
import NextPage from '../../components/NextPage.astro';
---

<!-- Every statement here comes from the Society's constitution (the version on the ACNC register, dated 2013). The four aims are a plain-language paraphrase of the constitution's objects; the original wording is in the linked PDF. -->
<BaseLayout
  title="Membership and governance"
  description="How The Sydney Society of The New Church is run, who is responsible, and what becoming a member involves."
  currentPath="/about/membership"
>
  <main class="container-prose px-6 md:px-8 pt-10 md:pt-14 pb-4">
    <div class="font-display font-semibold text-eyebrow tracking-eyebrow uppercase text-accent-primary mb-2.5">
      ✦ About us
    </div>

    <article class="prose-rnc">
      <h1>Membership and governance</h1>
      <p>
        This page explains what membership of The Sydney Society of The New Church means, how to join, and how the
        Society is run. The Society’s constitution is the authority, and you can read it in full at the bottom of this
        page.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3 not-prose">
        <Button href="/account/membership">Apply to become a member</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href="#check-membership">Check if you are a member</Button>
      </div>

      <h2 id="what-membership-means">What membership means</h2>
      <p>
        At heart, membership is about belonging. It means being part of our community, sharing in what we believe, and
        supporting the church and one another. Many people take part in the life of the church without being members,
        and they are always welcome. Membership is simply a way of making that belonging official.
      </p>
      <p>The constitution also gives membership a formal side. Open either box for the details.</p>

      <details class={box}>
        <summary class={summary}>
          What members can do
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 8l5 5 5-5" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <ul class={boxList + ' mt-4'}>
          <li><strong>Vote</strong> at general meetings, including the Annual General Meeting.</li>
          <li><strong>Serve on the Committee.</strong> Only members can.</li>
          <li><strong>Receive notice</strong> of general meetings, and the audited accounts before the AGM.</li>
          <li><strong>Call a special meeting</strong>, together with nine other members.</li>
          <li><strong>Attend</strong> the Society’s lectures and meetings and use its library and rooms, subject to any by-laws.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <details class={box}>
        <summary class={summary}>
          What it asks of members
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 8l5 5 5-5" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <ul class={boxList + ' mt-4'}>
          <li>To share the Society’s core beliefs (see <a href="#what-the-society-is-for">What the Society is for</a>, further down).</li>
          <li>
            To stand behind the Society in a small way. The Society is a company limited by guarantee, so if it were ever
            wound up each member may be asked to contribute <strong>{memberConfig.guarantee}</strong>, and nothing more,
            while a member or within a year after leaving. There are no fees or dues.
          </li>
          <li>To keep to the Society’s rules and by-laws, and not to act in a way that harms the Society’s interests.</li>
          <li>To let the Society keep their name and address on the roll of members.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <h2 id="becoming-a-member">How to become a member</h2>
      <p>Anyone is welcome to worship with us and join in. To become a member:</p>
      <ul class={list}>
        <li>You ask to join, and say that you broadly share the Society’s core beliefs: that Jesus Christ is the one God, that the Bible is sacred, that turning away from harmful ways is part of a life of faith, and that we are called to care for everyone.</li>
        <li>You agree to have your name entered on the roll of members.</li>
        <li>The Committee then makes it official, which is a formality. Two existing members put your name forward at a Committee meeting, one to propose you and one to second, and the Committee accepts you, taking the Minister’s advice into account.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can send your application through this website. Sign in, open <strong>Membership</strong> and follow the
        steps. It goes to the Committee at its next meeting.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you’d like to talk anything over first, such as what we believe or what membership involves, you’re welcome
        to have a chat with our Minister. You can{' '}
        <a href="mailto:pastor@rosevillenewchurch.com.au">send an email</a> or ask after a Sunday service. There’s no
        need to do this before you apply.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div id="check-membership" class="my-10 scroll-mt-24">
      <Card variant="tint" title="Check if you are a member">
        <p class="m-0 mb-3">
          Our records aren’t perfect, so it’s worth checking how we have you listed. It takes about a minute and there
          is no password.
        </p>
        <ol class="m-0 mb-4 pl-5 list-decimal flex flex-col gap-2">
          <li>Choose <strong>Log in</strong> below and enter the email address you use with the church.</li>
          <li>We email you a 6-digit code. Type it in on the next page.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Membership</strong> in your account. It tells you whether our records show you as a member.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="m-0 text-sm text-text-muted">
          If it doesn’t look right, or we don’t have your email address, <a href="/contact" class="text-link hover:text-link-hover">let us know</a> and we’ll put it right.
        </p>
        <div slot="footer"><Button size="sm" href="/login">Log in to check</Button></div>
      </Card>
    </div>

    <article class="prose-rnc">
      <h2>Leaving</h2>
      <p>
        You can resign at any time by sending a request from <strong>Membership</strong> on this website, and the
        Committee will deal with it at its next meeting. You stay on our list as a friend unless you ask us not to.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-we-are-run">How the Society is run</h2>
      <h3>Who we are</h3>
      <p>
        The Sydney Society of The New Church is the organisation behind Roseville New Church. It was established in 1875
        and incorporated in 1886. It is a company limited by guarantee (ACN 000 001 105, ABN 39 929 678 230) and a
        registered charity with the Australian Charities and Not-for-profits Commission (ACNC), advancing religion. Its
        registered office is 4 Shirley Rd, Roseville NSW 2069. You can read more of our story on the{' '}
        <a href="/about">Our church page</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="what-the-society-is-for">What the Society is for</h3>
      <p>
        The constitution sets four aims. Here they are in everyday words (the original wording is in the constitution
        linked at the bottom of this page):
      </p>
      <ul class={list}>
        <li>To worship Jesus Christ as the one God, who made us, sets us free and guides our spiritual growth.</li>
        <li>To read and study the Bible, and to explore its deeper spiritual meaning.</li>
        <li>To turn away from what does harm, and to live a life of selfless service and care for everyone.</li>
        <li>To share the writings of Emanuel Swedenborg, and other works that support the life and teachings of the New Church.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The Society does not pay dividends or distribute profits to its members: any surplus is applied to these
        objects. If it were ever wound up, whatever was left after its debts were paid would go to another institution
        with similar objects, not to members.
      </p>

      <h3>Who is responsible</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>The members</strong> make the big decisions at general meetings. They elect the Committee, appoint the
        Minister (or a Leader) and the auditor, and adopt the Society’s accounts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The Committee</strong> manages the Society’s affairs and exercises all of its powers except those the
        constitution keeps for a general meeting. It is made up of a Chairman of the Committee, a Secretary, a
        Treasurer and between four and seven other members, including the Minister, chosen by the members at the Annual
        General Meeting from among the members. It meets at least every three months and keeps minutes of its
        decisions. It can fill a vacancy until the next AGM and can make by-laws.
      </p>
      <ul class={list}>
        <li><strong>The Minister</strong> looks after worship, keeps the registers of baptisms, marriages and funerals, and sits on the Committee.</li>
        <li><strong>The Secretary</strong> handles correspondence, keeps the roll of members (names and addresses), gives members notice of general meetings and takes the minutes of them.</li>
        <li><strong>The Treasurer</strong> receives and accounts for the Society’s money and pays the accounts the Committee has approved.</li>
        <li><strong>The auditor</strong> examines the accounts and reports to the AGM.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To reach the Secretary or the Committee, please use the{' '}
        <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Meetings and accounts</h3>
      <p>
        The Annual General Meeting is held each February. It receives the year’s reports, adopts the accounts and elects
        the Committee. Members are given at least seven days’ notice of any general meeting. The Committee can call a
        special general meeting at any time, and ten members together can require one in writing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions are decided by a majority of the members present, and the chairman has a casting vote only. Voting is
        by show of hands unless a member asks for a poll, and proxies are allowed only for electing officers. The
        accounts are closed on 30 September, audited at least fourteen days before the AGM, and a copy goes to each
        member beforehand.
      </p>

      <h2>Documents</h2>
      <ul class={list}>
        <li><a href="/documents/sydney-society-constitution.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Society’s constitution (PDF)</a>, the version registered with the ACNC.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.acnc.gov.au/charity/charities/f2aa6689-38af-e811-a95e-000d3ad24c60/documents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Society on the ACNC charity register</a>, with its public documents and financial reports.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <NextPage title="Swedenborg Centre" href="/about/swedenborg-centre" label="About the Swedenborg Centre">
      The Society’s online service for exploring Swedenborg’s writings, with talks, discussion and study groups open
      to everyone. And if you have questions about membership or how the Society works, the Committee is glad to talk
      them through.
      <Fragment slot="more">
        <Button variant="secondary" href="/contact">Ask the Committee</Button>
        <Button variant="ghost" href="/events/sunday-worship">Plan a Sunday visit</Button>
      </Fragment>
    </NextPage>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
